//! The conjugations, commonest first, each named after a word that follows it.
//!
//! A pattern is the form with `~` where the stem goes: `~а` is the stem and
//! `а`; a stem in two pieces, `~о~`, has a vowel between them that other
//! forms drop. Alternatives are separated by `|`, the usual one first.

use flexion::Paradigm;

/// Writes one conjugation: the word it is named after, how many words follow it,
/// the lemma's pattern, its words' inherent features (`[gender: Neuter]`, if
/// they share them), the conjugation it is like (if any), then a row of patterns
/// per line (`derived`: the form comes from another cell's, see
/// `relations.rs`; `_`: the form is the conjugation's it is like):
/// `indicative_active_present_first` (sg, pl)
/// `indicative_active_present_second` (sg, pl)
/// `indicative_active_present_third` (sg, pl)
/// `indicative_active_imperfect_first` (sg, pl)
/// `indicative_active_imperfect_second` (sg, pl)
/// `indicative_active_imperfect_third` (sg, pl)
/// `indicative_active_future_first` (sg, pl)
